  {
   "op_code": "0xE5",
   "size": 1,
   "text": "Write current_value to {}",
   "param_index": [],
   "complexity": "complex",
   "param_type": [],
   "param_list": [],
   "short_text": "Store value to var",
   "func_name": "store_var",
   "comment": "Verified via decompile (2026-09): each parameter is a single store into the entity running the sequence; nothing is queued and nothing is masked (only the C3 read side masks). Notes on 0x17 (Y rotation), the one that surprises people: it writes based_rotation_y (entity +0x0E) ABSOLUTELY - there is no rotate-by opcode, compute the new angle and store it again. 4096 = a full turn; the neutral facing is 0 for party slots and 2048 (180 degrees) for monsters (resetBasedRotation 0x502070). The renderer never reads the field directly: pre_pre_linkedToAnimationSequence (0x502AB0) rebuilds the entity matrix from it EVERY tick via ComposeZYXRotationMatrix, so the write is level-triggered - it lands next frame and holds until something overwrites it. What overwrites it: opcode A2 (end of sequence) and AC (restore base model) reset it to neutral, and so does 92 (setup targeting) when the target is self or multiple - but 92 with a single target other than self instead SNAPS the facing to that target (based_rotation_y = CalculateAngleBetweenXZ(attacker, target), AnimSeq92_SetupTargetingContext 0x50D300). So write 0x17 AFTER any 92, never before. Two cases where the value is stored but not seen: with combat flag TURN_BACK (opcode 9C) the renderer applies ApplyYRotation(angleToCamera - based_rotation_y - 1024), making the field a negative offset from the camera angle rather than the facing; and on a part linked into a multi-part chain (opcode 90) the chain head's whole matrix block is copied over this entity's, discarding its rotation. E5 0x31 is the same write aimed at the current target. Axis note: the three angle fields are applied X (+0x0C), Y (+0x0E), Z (+0x10), Y last (outermost, a world yaw); the exe's own field names for +0x0C and +0x10 are swapped relative to the axis they drive, the 0x30/0x31/0x32 names used here are the correct ones."
  },
